                  { elem: "FG", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "D : AB = E² : FG²", ref: "X.6.Cor" }] },
            { text: "Then the square on {E} is commensurable with the square on {FG}.",
              visible: FGL,
              highlighted: ["E","FG"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "E², FG² commensurable", ref: "X.6" }] },
            { text: "And {E} is rational, therefore {FG} is also rational.",
              visible: FGL,
              highlighted: ["E","FG"] },
            { text: "And, since {D} does not have to {AB} the ratio which a square number has to a square number,",
              visible: FGL,
              highlighted: ["D","AB"] },
            { text: "neither does the square on {E} have to the square on {FG} the ratio which a square number has to a square number,",
              visible: FGL,
              highlighted: ["E","FG"] },
            { text: "therefore {E} is incommensurable in length with {FG}.",
              visible: FGL,
              highlighted: ["E","FG"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "E, FG incommensurable in length", ref: "X.9" }] },
            { text: "Next let it be contrived that the number {BA} is to {AC} as the square on {FG} is to the square on {GH}.",
              visible: GHL,
              highlighted: ["AB","AC","FG","GH"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "GH", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "BA : AC = FG² : GH²", ref: "X.6.Cor" }] },
            { text: "Then the square on {FG} is commensurable with the square on {GH}.",
              visible: GHL,
              highlighted: ["FG","GH"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "FG², GH² commensurable", ref: "X.6" }] },
            { text: "But {FG} is rational, therefore {GH} is also rational.",
              visible: GHL,
              highlighted: ["FG","GH"] },
            { text: "And, since {BA} does not have to {AC} the ratio which a square number has to a square number,",
              visible: GHL,
              highlighted: ["AB","AC"] },
            { text: "neither does the square on {FG} have to the square on {HG} the ratio which a square number has to a square number,",
              visible: GHL,
              highlighted: ["FG","GH"] },
            { text: "therefore {FG} is incommensurable in length with {GH}.",
              visible: GHL,
              highlighted: ["FG","GH"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "FG, GH incommensurable in length", ref: "X.9" }] },
            { text: "Therefore {FG} and {GH} are rational straight lines commensurable in square only. Therefore {FH} is binomial.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["FH"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "FH binomial", ref: "X.36" }] },
            { text: "I say next that it is also a third binomial straight line.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["FH"] },
            { text: "Since {D} is to {AB} as the square on {E} is to the square on {FG},",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["D","AB","E","FG"] },
            { text: "and {BA} is to {AC} as the square on {FG} is to the square on {GH},",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["AB","AC","FG","GH"] },
            { text: "therefore, ex aequali, {D} is to {AC} as the square on {E} is to the square on {GH}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["D","AC","E","GH"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "D : AC = E² : GH²", ref: "V.22" }] },
            { text: "But {D} does not have to {AC} the ratio which a square number has to a square number,",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["D","AC"] },
            { text: "therefore neither does the square on {E} have to the square on {GH} the ratio which a square number has to a square number.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["E","GH"] },
            { text: "Therefore {E} is incommensurable in length with {GH}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["E","GH"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "E, GH incommensurable in length", ref: "X.9" }] },
            { text: "Since {BA} is to {AC} as the square on {FG} is to the square on {GH},",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["AB","AC","FG","GH"] },
            { text: "therefore the square on {FG} is greater than the square on {GH}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["FG","GH"] },
            { text: "Let then the sum of the squares on {GH} and K equal the square on {FG}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["GH","FG"] },
            { text: "Then, in conversion, {AB} is to {BC} as the square on {FG} is to the square on K.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["AB","CB","FG"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "AB : BC = FG² : K²", ref: "V.19.Cor" }] },
            { text: "But {AB} has to {BC} the ratio which a square number has to a square number,",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["AB","CB"] },
            { text: "therefore the square on {FG} also has to the square on K the ratio which a square number has to a square number.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["FG"] },
            { text: "Therefore {FG} is commensurable in length with K.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["FG"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "FG, K commensurable in length", ref: "X.9" }] },
            { text: "Therefore the square on {FG} is greater than the square on {GH} by the square on a straight line commensurable with {FG}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["FG","GH"] },
            { text: "And {FG} and {GH} are rational straight lines commensurable in square only,",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["FG","GH"] },
            { text: "and neither of them is commensurable in length with {E}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["FG","GH","E"] },
            { text: "Therefore {FH} is a third binomial straight line.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["FH"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "X.50") return "#prop";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "V.19.Cor": "/elements/books/bookV/propositions/propV19/#cor",
        "V.22":     "/elements/books/bookV/propositions/propV22/",
        "X.6":      "/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX6/",
        "X.6.Cor":  "/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX6/#cor",
        "X.9":      "/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX9/",
        "X.36":     "/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX36/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

And {E} is rational, therefore {FG} is also rational. And, since {D} does not have to {AB} the ratio which a square number has to a square number, neither does the square on {E} have to the square on {FG} the ratio which a square number has to a square number, therefore {E} is incommensurable in length with {FG}. [!just X.9]

Next let it be contrived that the number {BA} is to {AC} as the square on {FG} is to the square on {GH}. Then the square on {FG} is commensurable with the square on {GH}. [!just X.6.Cor; X.6]

But {FG} is rational, therefore {GH} is also rational. And, since {BA} does not have to {AC} the ratio which a square number has to a square number, neither does the square on {FG} have to the square on {HG} the ratio which a square number has to a square number, therefore {FG} is incommensurable in length with {GH}. [!just X.9]

Therefore {FG} and {GH} are rational straight lines commensurable in square only. Therefore {FH} is binomial. [!just X.36]

I say next that it is also a third binomial straight line.

Since {D} is to {AB} as the square on {E} is to the square on {FG}, and {BA} is to {AC} as the square on {FG} is to the square on {GH}, therefore, *ex aequali*, {D} is to {AC} as the square on {E} is to the square on {GH}. [!just V.22]

But {D} does not have to {AC} the ratio which a square number has to a square number, therefore neither does the square on {E} have to the square on {GH} the ratio which a square number has to a square number. Therefore {E} is incommensurable in length with {GH}. [!just X.9]

Since {BA} is to {AC} as the square on {FG} is to the square on {GH}, therefore the square on {FG} is greater than the square on {GH}.

Let then the sum of the squares on {GH} and *K* equal the square on {FG}. Then, in conversion, {AB} is to {BC} as the square on {FG} is to the square on *K*. [!just V.19.Cor]

But {AB} has to {BC} the ratio which a square number has to a square number, therefore the square on {FG} also has to the square on *K* the ratio which a square number has to a square number. Therefore {FG} is commensurable in length with *K*. [!just X.9]

Therefore the square on {FG} is greater than the square on {GH} by the square on a straight line commensurable with {FG}.

And {FG} and {GH} are rational straight lines commensurable in square only, and neither of them is commensurable in length with {E}.

Therefore {FH} is a third binomial straight line.

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
